# TEMPLATE — cargo NEVER reads this file. It reads `.cargo/config.toml` (or
# `.cargo/config`), and this repo's `.gitignore` ignores that live file precisely
# so a local override can never be committed. To use this: `cp
# .cargo/config.toml.example .cargo/config.toml`, then adjust the path.
#
# WHAT IT IS FOR. `src-tauri/Cargo.toml` depends on `fulcrum-core` as a GIT
# dependency pinned to the `v0.1.0` tag (with `version = "0.1.0"`), and the crate's
# sources are NOT in this checkout any more (they live in
# https://github.com/archimedes-codes/fulcrum).
# So editing the engine means editing a file you cannot see from here. If you are
# working on the desktop AND the engine at the same time, this redirects the git
# source at a sibling checkout, so `cargo build` compiles YOUR engine code and a
# change you make there shows up in the desktop's build immediately.
#
# WHY `[patch]` AND NOT `[paths]`. Cargo's older `[paths]` override is deprecated
# and, decisively here, it only rewrites PATH dependencies by package name — it
# cannot express a GIT source, so it silently does nothing to a pinned git dep.
# `[patch]` is the mechanism that can: it replaces a package coming from a named
# SOURCE (here, that git URL) with a different one, and the table name is the
# source URL being overridden, which must match the dependency's URL exactly
# (same scheme, same host, same repo path — `https://`, not `git@`, and no
# trailing `.git` difference) or cargo reports the patch as unused.
#
# ⚠️ SINCE THE PIN GREW A `version`, THE SIBLING CHECKOUT MUST MATCH IT. This is a
# NEW failure mode and it is the SILENT one: `version = "0.1.0"` in the dependency
# is a REQUIREMENT, so `[patch]` only applies if the patched manifest satisfies it.
# Point it at a checkout whose `Cargo.toml` still says `0.11.0` (any commit before
# the v0.1.0 bump) and cargo prints
#     warning: patch `fulcrum-core v0.11.0 (/path/to/fulcrum)` was not used in the crate graph
# and then BUILDS THE GIT DEP ANYWAY — exit 0, no error, and your engine edits are
# simply not in the binary. Measured both ways on this tree: a sibling at the
# pre-bump rev printed that warning and `cargo tree` kept showing the git source;
# the same sibling checked out at the tagged commit (`0.1.0`) patched cleanly. So
# the warning is not informational, it is the whole failure — read it. The fix is
# to check the sibling out at or past the bump commit, or to have the sibling carry
# a matching `version` while you work there.
#
# ⚠️ THIS FILE IS FOR LOCAL WORK ONLY — NEVER COMMIT THE LIVE VERSION.
# A committed patch would (a) break every other machine, which has no `../fulcrum`
# directory, with `failed to load source for dependency on fulcrum-core`, and
# (b) defeat the pin: the build would use whatever that tree happens to hold
# instead of the tagged commit. That is the exact failure this pin exists to
# prevent, so `.gitignore` ignores `.cargo/config.toml` and the pin stays the
# only thing that reaches CI.
#
# HOW TO TELL IT IS ACTUALLY WORKING. `cargo tree -i fulcrum-core` prints the
# patched source as a PATH, e.g.
#     fulcrum-core v0.1.0 (/path/to/fulcrum)
# rather than the `git+https://…?tag=…` form. If it still prints the git URL, the
# patch is NOT in effect — the two causes are the `[patch]` table name missing the
# dependency URL character-for-character, and the version mismatch above. Cargo
# prints `Patch … was not used in the crate graph` for either; do not ignore it.
#
# A SECOND CLEANUP, SPECIFIC TO THE VERSION MISMATCH. When the patch DOES apply,
# `Cargo.lock` rewrites `fulcrum-core`'s `source` to the path (and drops the
# `source = "git+…"` line). That is why this file must stay gitignored AND why you
# must `git checkout Cargo.lock` after deleting `.cargo/config.toml` — the lock
# change survives the deletion and, unlike the config, the lock IS tracked. Verify
# with `cargo tree -i fulcrum-core`: it must print the `git+https://…?tag=…` form
# again before you commit anything.
#
# HOW TO UNDO IT: delete `.cargo/config.toml`, then `git checkout Cargo.lock`.
#
# ⚠️ THE TABLE NAME IS `[patch."…"]`, WITH THE DOT AND THE QUOTES. `[patch "…"]`
# (a space, no dot) is NOT valid TOML and cargo dies with
# `could not parse TOML configuration … unclosed table, expected ']'` — which is a
# hard failure of EVERY cargo command in the repo, not a silent no-op. The dotted
# form is what cargo documents: the quoted string is a KEY of the `[patch]` table
# naming the source being overridden. (Measured: the space form was tried first and
# broke `cargo tree` outright.)

[patch."https://github.com/archimedes-codes/fulcrum"]
fulcrum-core = { path = "../fulcrum" }
